  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery reveals widespread mid-level \ncloudiness across eastern Idaho as the region remains embedded \nin a moist, southwesterly flow regime. Widespread showers with \nembedded thunderstorms continue to move through, delivering \nbeneficial wetting rainfall across much of the area. The primary\noperational impact today is heavy rain over recent burn scars; \na Flash Flood Warning remains in effect for the Big Rock Burn \nScar, where heavy rainfall has already produced active flooding.\nOutside of the burn scar, rainfall totals have been more \nmodest. Temperatures are substantially colder than 24 hours ago,\nwith much of the region currently sitting in the 60s under \ncloud cover and precipitation. High-resolution convective- \nallowing models (CAMs) indicate another surge of moisture \nthrough late evening before activity diminishes overnight to \nisolated or scattered coverage.\n\nA second, weaker shortwave impulse arrives from the southwest \non Friday. Because this system lacks the strength of today's \ndisturbance, convective coverage will be less widespread. \nGuidance heavily favors the central mountains and higher terrain\ncloser to the storm track, though showers and isolated storms \nremain likely across lower valley zones. With monsoonal moisture\nremaining entrenched, locally heavy rainfall will stay a \nthreat, prompting a Flash Flood Watch for the Wapiti Burn Scar \nstarting Friday afternoon and continuing through Saturday \nevening.\n\nOn Saturday, one final shortwave disturbance tracks through the\nregion as upper-level flow flattens from southwesterly to \nwesterly. Model guidance has trended higher with precipitation \nprobabilities (PoPs) area-wide, making another day of scattered \nto widespread showers and thunderstorms likely regionwide. The \ncentral mountains remain the primary target for storm activity, \nand a localized flash flood threat could linger through \nSaturday. Temperatures throughout this period will remain well \non the cool side of normal under persistent cloud cover and \nrain.\n\nA transition toward drier conditions takes hold Sunday into\nearly next week, though residual low- and mid-level moisture\nwill linger. Because the ridge cap building over the region is\nunlikely to be strong enough to completely suppress convection,\nisolated diurnal (heat-of-the-day) pop-up thunderstorms are\nexpected each afternoon and evening. Daytime highs will moderate\nback into the lower 80s by next week, remaining just a few\ndegrees below seasonal averages.",
